          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Since my knee replacement I have had calf pain and the leg looks swollen. How urgent is that, and what does guidance describe about the risk?",
          "answer": "If your symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, you should seek medical advice [condition-osteoarthritis, guideline-osteoarthritis-management, condition-iron-deficiency-anemia, condition-heart-failure, condition-upper-respiratory-infection].\n\nRegarding urgency, the calf pain and swelling following knee replacement is routinely assessed, and no emergency pattern in the red-flag table matched this description [condition-osteoarthritis, guideline-osteoarthritis-management, condition-iron-deficiency-anemia, condition-heart-failure, condition-upper-respiratory-infection]. However, this assessment is not a clearance, as the table covers a fixed list of presentations, and a symptom it does not list can still be serious [condition-osteoarthritis, guideline-osteoarthritis-management, condition-iron-deficiency-anemia, condition-heart-failure, condition-upper-respiratory-infection].\n\nIn contrast, other guidance suggests that you should seek immediate medical attention if you experience these symptoms, as calf pain and swelling after knee replacement can be a sign of a serious condition such as deep vein thrombosis (DVT) [guideline-osteoarthritis-management, condition-osteoarthritis, condition-iron-deficiency-anemia, condition-upper-respiratory-infection, condition-coronary-artery-disease, condition-heart-failure, condition-hypothyroidism, coding-icd10-chapter-09-circulatory, lifestyle-type-2-diabetes-activity, red-flag-anaphylaxis]. While this guidance does not go into extensive detail about the specific risks, it highlights the importance of urgent assessment for symptoms that could indicate complications like DVT [guideline-osteoarthritis-management, condition-osteoarthritis, condition-iron-deficiency-anemia, condition-upper-respiratory-infection, condition-coronary-artery-disease, condition-heart-failure, condition-hypothyroidism, coding-icd10-chapter-09-circulatory, lifestyle-type-2-diabetes-activity, red-flag-anaphylaxis]. DVT is a type of venous thromboembolism (VTE) involving blood clots that often form in deep veins, typically in the legs, and can lead to serious issues, including pulmonary embolism if a clot travels to the lungs [guideline-osteoarthritis-management, condition-osteoarthritis, condition-iron-deficiency-anemia, condition-upper-respiratory-infection, condition-coronary-artery-disease, condition-heart-failure, condition-hypothyroidism, coding-icd10-chapter-09-circulatory, lifestyle-type-2-diabetes-activity, red-flag-anaphylaxis].\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
